d01", "start": 400, "end": 559, "text": "By the time he graduated from Lackey High School, he could prepare a wide variety of dishes, ranging from classic French cuisine to contemporary fusion dishes."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0209-0f350c9aa919:d01", "start": 560, "end": 680, "text": "Although his primary focus was on molecular gastronomy, he was already catering events and working in local restaurants."}, "D3S1": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0209-0f350c9aa919:d02", "start": 0, "end": 256, "text": "Greg Boyer (water polo) Gregory Vaitl \"Greg\" Boyer (born February 5, 1958) is a former American water polo player who was a member of the United States men's national water polo team and won a silver medal at the 1988 Summer Olympics in Seoul, South Korea."}, "D3S2": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0209-0f350c9aa919:d02", "start": 257, "end": 334, "text": "Boyer grew up in New York City and played water polo at Aviation High School."}, "D3S3": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0209-0f350c9aa919:d02", "start": 335, "end": 459, "text": "He attended the University of California, Santa Barbara where he played on the Gauchos men's water polo team from 1976–1979."}, "D3S4": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0209-0f350c9aa919:d02", "start": 460, "end": 532, "text": "He was a three-time All-American, being named to the first team in 1979."}, "D3S5": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0209-0f350c9aa919:d02", "start": 533, "end": 562, "text": "The Gauchos won the 1979 NCAA"}, "D4S1": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0209-0f350c9aa919:d03", "start": 0, "end": 18, "text": "7 Mouchawar 8 Je ."}, "D4S2": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0209-0f350c9aa919:d03", "start": 19, "end": 62, "text": "Campbell 9 Boyer 10 Schroeder ( C ) 11 Jo ."}, "D4S3": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0209-0f350c9aa919:d03", "start": 63, "end": 174, "text": "Campbell 12 Duplanty ( GK ) 13 Evans Coach : Barne